      <data key="d3">The 20-year term defines the coverage window, but the allocation of benefits to Emma depends on the contingency clause involving surviving beneficiaries, not the term itself.</data>
      <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="b9bffd3e-9339-46c9-88e5-c86723b048a8" target="2cc79eb8-7783-467b-b2ad-cad32efd2a0c">
      <data key="d3">The 20-year policy term defines the coverage window, while Emma's status as the insured’s daughter establishes her identity as a potential contingent beneficiary under that same policy.</data>
      <data key="d4">CONTEXTUALIZES</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="b9bffd3e-9339-46c9-88e5-c86723b048a8" target="53ad6f3d-85c7-46d3-8a96-bb170408cdd8">
      <data key="d3">The 20-year term defines the coverage window during which benefits may be paid, but it does not determine or influence the naming of contingent beneficiaries such as Emma Grace Anderson.</data>
      <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="b9bffd3e-9339-46c9-88e5-c86723b048a8" target="61095de3-b57e-4b5b-ba01-9a506f62acd3">
      <data key="d3">The 20-year term defines the coverage period during which the policy remains effective, enabling provisions such as contingent beneficiary designations—like Lucas—to apply if certain conditions occur.</data>
      <data key="d4">CONTEXTUAL_DEPENDENCE</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
